('A 30% drop in the first three years turns a $40,000 withdrawal into ~6%; retiring in 2000 hit the dot-com crash and financial crisis within nine years.','If the market\ndrops 30% in your first 3 years','K02: 40,000 / 700,000 = 5.7% (reproduces). The 2000 retiree example is a standard sequence-risk illustration.',[]),
('Bengen\'s 1994 study found 4.15%; he raised it to 4.5% then 4.7% (now a worst-case floor), says 5-5.5% fine normally; Morningstar\'s forward-looking analysis is 3.9%; $39,000 vs $47,000 is a 20% raise.','ran the study in 1994','Consistent with Bengen (1994; 2025 book) and Morningstar\'s 2025 safe withdrawal research at 3.9% (analyst recall, not fetched). K02: 47/39 = +20.5% (reproduces).',[]),
